# Only two cusps on X₀(ℓ)_ℚ̄ at prime level

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.eq_cuspInftyBar_or_eq_cuspZeroBar`
(`838f6e71-e7c0-5969-aef1-705a3042b5af`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `dbf3629f-a03b-5832-8812-b9a975b556ab`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $\ell$ be a prime and let $\bar F_\ell$ denote `modularFunctionFieldBar ℓ`, the intermediate
field of $\bar{\mathbb Q}((q))$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ generated by the coefficientwise image under
`coeffEmb` of `modularFunctionFieldFull ℓ` (itself the subfield of $\mathbb Q((q))$ generated over
$\mathbb Q$ by the set `divisorExpansions ℓ`). Write $\bar j$ for the element of $\bar F_\ell$
obtained by applying `coeffEmb` to the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{ofPowerSeries}(jNumQ)$,
i.e. the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$. Let $w$ be a place of $\bar F_\ell$ over
$\bar{\mathbb Q}$, that is, a valuation subring of $\bar F_\ell$ which is not the whole field,
contains the image of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, and is a principal ideal ring. Assume $\bar j$ is not in
the valuation subring of $w$, which is the meaning of `IsCusp` for $\bar j$ and $w$. Then $w$ equals
either `cuspInftyBar ℓ`, the place whose valuation subring consists of the elements of non-negative
$q$-order, or `cuspZeroBar ℓ`, its translate under the Fricke involution `frickeInvolutionBar ℓ`.

This is the classical statement that $X_0(\ell)$ over an algebraically closed field of
characteristic zero has exactly two cusps, $\infty$ and $0$, here in the form: any place of the
function field at which $j$ has a pole is one of these two. It is used throughout the analysis of
places of $\bar F_\ell$ and their specialisations, in particular by the level-one prolongation
lemmas on divisors and residues.

